INSTALLATION

Edisyn uses coremidi4j and jack-midi-spi.  You don't need to install them, they're
part of Edisyn's jar file and executable.



DISTRIBUTION

The Jar File coremidi4j-1.1.jar is distributed under the Eclipse Common License.
Edisyn does not modify or extend it, but just links against it.  If you would like
the source code to this jar file, you're in luck!  It's embedded within the jar
file itself, in a subdirectory called "source".

The CoreMidi4J distribution is maintained here:
	https://github.com/DerekCook/CoreMidi4J

I'd go there for the most recent copies.



jack-midi-spi-1.0.0.jar, jnajack-1.5.1.jar, and jna-5.13.0.jar together add a
javax.sound.midi Service Provider that exposes JACK/PipeWire MIDI ports as regular
MIDI devices on Linux (see https://github.com/eclab/edisyn/issues/116). Edisyn does
not modify these jars, it just links against them. All three are LGPL/Apache 2.0
licensed. Note jack-midi-spi's own published pom.xml depends on org.jaudiolibs's
official jnajack; that version is missing a ByteBuffer overload of
JackMidi.eventWrite() that jack-midi-spi actually calls, causing a NoSuchMethodError
and no MIDI data flowing. You must use the casa.squid fork of jnajack (1.5.1 or
later) instead, which is what's vendored here.

	https://github.com/squidcasa/jack-midi-spi
	https://github.com/squidcasa/jnajack
	https://github.com/java-native-access/jna

I'd go there for the most recent copies.
